From the `Keyof Type Operator` handbook page:

1. What `keyof` does
- The page states: “The `keyof` operator takes an object type and produces a string or numeric literal union of its keys.”
- First example:
  ```ts
  type Point = { x: number; y: number };
  type P = keyof Point;
  // type P = "x" | "y"
  ```
  So `keyof Point` evaluates to the union of property names, here the string literal union `"x" | "y"`.

- Second example (index signatures):
  ```ts
  type Arrayish = { [n: number]: unknown };
  type A = keyof Arrayish;
  // type A = number

  type Mapish = { [k: string]: boolean };
  type M = keyof Mapish;
  // type M = string | number
  ```
  The text clarifies that if the type has a string or number index signature, `keyof` returns those types instead. In the `Mapish` case, `M` becomes `string | number` because JavaScript object keys are always coerced to strings, so `obj[0]` is the same as `obj["0"]`.

2. How `keyof` relates to `Pick` and `Omit` (per docs examples and their behavior)
From the Utility Types reference (visited in earlier steps):

- `Pick<Type, Keys>`
  - Generic signature (from the Utility Types page):
    ```ts
    Pick<Type, Keys>
    ```
    where `Keys` is constrained to `keyof Type`.
  - Conceptually, `Keys` must come from the set produced by `keyof Type`. That is, you use `keyof` to describe which property names are valid to “pick” from `Type`.
  - The first example on that page uses:
    ```ts
    interface Todo {
      title: string;
      description: string;
      completed: boolean;
    }

    type TodoPreview = Pick<Todo, "title" | "completed">;
    ```
    Here, `"title" | "completed"` is a subset of `keyof Todo` (which is `"title" | "description" | "completed"`). The result type `TodoPreview` keeps only the properties whose names are in that `keyof`-derived subset.
  - Relation to `keyof`: `Pick`’s second type parameter is a union of keys taken from `keyof` the first parameter. So `keyof` defines the space of allowed property-name unions, and `Pick` forms a new type by including only properties whose names are in that union.

- `Omit<Type, Keys>`
  - Generic signature (from the Utility Types page):
    ```ts
    Omit<Type, Keys>
    ```
    Typically defined in the standard library as:
    ```ts
    type Omit<T, K extends keyof any> = Pick<T, Exclude<keyof T, K>>;
    ```
    This shows the relationship explicitly: `Omit` uses `keyof T` to get all keys, then removes `K` via `Exclude`, and finally uses `Pick` on the remaining keys.
  - The first example on that page uses something like:
    ```ts
    interface Todo {
      title: string;
      description: string;
      completed: boolean;
      createdAt: number;
    }

    type TodoPreview = Omit<Todo, "description" | "createdAt">;
    ```
    Here:
    - `keyof Todo` is the union of all property names on `Todo`.
    - `"description" | "createdAt"` is a subset of that union.
    - `Omit` conceptually computes `keyof Todo` minus that subset and then keeps only the remaining keys.
  - Relation to `keyof`: `Omit` is literally defined in terms of `keyof` in the docs’ type-level definition and examples. It uses `keyof` to enumerate all property keys of `Type`, then removes the keys specified by its second type argument.

Summary of relationship according to the docs:
- `keyof` produces a union of property names (or index key types) from an object type.
- `Pick` takes a union of keys that are drawn from `keyof Type` and forms a new object type containing only those properties.
- `Omit` uses `keyof` (often via `Exclude`) to subtract a set of keys from the full `keyof Type`, then uses `Pick` on the remaining keys. So, in the handbook’s examples, `keyof` is the foundational operator that supplies the key unions that `Pick` and `Omit` work with.

